[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/edc4379a-6788-4bba-9e17-c1bd26ba814e/chapters/1436a955-ae9a-49bd-9741-283e27638a63/studies/4fe1ea58-d952-4b1f-b1da-b154a11156cc/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Englund Gambit! Now, against 1. d4, you've got a surprise weapon with the Englund! Not particularly sound, and even with a variation where you trade a queen for not quite enough material, this opening can get tricky in shorter time controls and against opponents who aren't well-prepared.} 1...e5 2.dxe5 {White accepts it, obviously. We proceed with rapid development.} 2...Nc6 3.Nf3 {White tries to hold onto the pawn on e5. In the beginning, our play revolves around trying to grab it back.} 3...Qe7 4.Bf4 {White adds another defender. But that was sort of the plan! Because we do have a plan. In any case, they'll defend the pawn like this 34% of the time, which is more than 3.2 million games. We give check with the queen, also hitting the f4-bishop and the b2-pawn.} 4...Qb4+ 5.Bd2 {White blocks and attacks our queen 60% of the time. We capture on b2.} 5...Qxb2 6.Bc3 {This move attacks our queen and defends the rook, and it's played 24% of the time It seems attractive to attack the queen in the corner, but this gives us a winning advantage. We bring our bishop into the game, with a decisive pin!} 6...Bb4 7.Bxb4 {Choosing to exchange pieces to get rid of the pressure.} 7...Nxb4 {But we're simply crashing through on c2.} *

