[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/edc4379a-6788-4bba-9e17-c1bd26ba814e/chapters/1436a955-ae9a-49bd-9741-283e27638a63/studies/4fe1ea58-d952-4b1f-b1da-b154a11156cc/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Englund Gambit! Now, against 1. d4, you've got a surprise weapon with the Englund! Not particularly sound, and even with a variation where you trade a queen for not quite enough material, this opening can get tricky in shorter time controls and against opponents who aren't well-prepared.} 1...e5 2.dxe5 {White accepts it, obviously. We proceed with rapid development.} 2...Nc6 3.Nf3 {White tries to hold onto the pawn on e5. In the beginning, our play revolves around trying to grab it back.} 3...Qe7 4.Nc3 {White just develops a piece 21% of the time. This allows us to take on e5.} 4...Nxe5 5.e4 {White places a pawn in the center. We'll take measures to prevent the potentially irritating Nd5.} 5...c6 6.Be2 {The bishop is developed. We'll make room for the c8-bishop to get into the game.} 6...d6 7.O-O {White castles.} 7...g6 {And we're continuing with standard development, soon castling short.} *